    "chunk_text": "Instead, the framework relies on decentralised PicoDC edge networks\u2014ultra-low-power computing nodes running on solar panels and battery storage that place no additional demand on the grid.\n\nThe nodes will be co-located at renewable power sites to create \u201cCognitive Power Plants\u201d that sell computing capacity alongside electricity.\n\nThe app ships with a 4.5 gigabyte entry model, with larger models loading silently in the background. Somaratne said the engine reaches 140 to 160 tokens per second on a standard MacBook, five to seven times the roughly 20 tokens per second typical when open-weight models run on the same machine.\n\nAn Engram Scholar Edition for the school students, free of behavioural tracking, is planned for a later phase.\n\nSomaratne said Sovereign Stack has submitted three open standards to the IETF, W3C and IEEE. They cover how models are packaged, how inference engines read them and how the users can verify a model\u2019s creator, check for tampering and optionally, see what data it was trained on. He said this could help the creators identify models built on their copyrighted work.\n\nThe rollout begins on Apple macOS, followed by Linux, with a Windows version targeted within two to three months. Heavier workloads will be handled by the PicoDC network at affordable per-task prices.\n\nAnthosa and Sovereign Stack are setting up a Sri Lankan operating entity to deploy the infrastructure, keeping spending onshore apart from the initial hardware fabrication.\n\nWhile the first PicoDC hardware will be imported, Sovereign Stack aims to set up the local \u201clast-mile assembly\u201d operations to build engineering capacity and stimulate domestic manufacturing.\n\nSomaratne cautioned that reliance on the offshore AI platforms threatens national competitiveness.\n\n\u201cIf we keep outsourcing our thinking to frontier models, you\u2019re going to collapse the whole world into what I call statistical mediocrity,\u201d he warned."
