    "chunk_text": "Keerthi Rathna, a paddy farmer, said his harvest had failed. \u201cThe plants died just when it was time for the grains to grow,\u201d he said.\n\nBringing some relief to the villagers, local donors started bringing bottled waters to the village. Unfortunately, the water distributed by the government does not reach the village.\n\nNot far from Kadurugaswewa, in Meegaswewa, Afnas and his wife were catching Hungas (Stinging Catfish) to feed their family. The dried-up reservoir is an ideal place to catch these fish that live in mud.\n\n\u201cOur paddy fields were destroyed. We can\u2019t even get enough milk from cows. There\u2019s nothing much left for us now. These Hungas will keep my family fed for a few days,\u201d he said.\n\nBefore Afnas was able to take care of his family, but now he says, he digs in mud to find food.\n\nHungas, fish that live in mud, one of the last remaining sources of food\n\nKeerthi Rathna near his paddy field that has withered without water\n\nBandara Manike points at her well that\u2019s now dried up, with just mud and debris left\n\nW. A. Nadeesha carries water to her household. Villagers and animals use the same \u201cwater hole\u201d\n\nVillagers catching fish that live in mud, one of the only sources of food that remains\n\nVillagers catching the last remaining fish in a dried-up reservoir in Polonnaruwa"
