    "md5": "42b2b12ecf7f5f0039c72c32b9191867",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2003,
    "chunk_text": "More national parks may close as heat wave threatens wildlife\n\nColombo, Sep. 14 (Daily Mirror) - More national parks could be temporarily closed if the prevailing heatwave and prolonged dry conditions intensify, as wildlife authorities struggle to protect animals from increasingly harsh weather conditions.\n\nThe warning comes after the Wildlife Department decided to close Blocks 1 and 2 of Yala National Park from September 14 to October 15, while keeping Blocks 3, 4, 5 and 6 open to visitors.\n\nA senior Wildlife Department official said authorities were closely monitoring the weather and could consider temporarily closing additional national parks if temperatures continue to rise, particularly under an El Ni\u00f1o-related weather pattern.\n\nThe official said the prevailing heat and dry conditions were creating increasingly difficult conditions for wildlife, particularly in areas where water sources were drying up.\n\n\u201cAlthough we cannot control the prevailing temperatures, we can provide water and other essential facilities to help wildlife cope with the conditions,\u201d the official said.\n\nThe official said any decision to close additional parks would be based on prevailing weather conditions and the need to protect wildlife and their habitats.\n\nThe possible expansion of park closures comes at a time when Sri Lanka's wildlife tourism sector is generating billions of rupees in revenue.\n\nAccording to official figures, national parks and other wildlife attractions recorded 1,381,079 visitors between January and June 2026, generating Rs.4,279,609,307.37 in revenue.\n\nOf the total visitors, 759,411 were locals, while 621,668 were foreigners. The foreign visitors included 615,022 tourists from outside the SAARC region and 6,646 visitors from SAARC countries.\n\nYala National Park recorded the highest number of visitors during the six-month period, with more than 459,000 arrivals, followed by Udawalawe with 219,465, Horton Plains with 196,651 and the Elephant Transit Home with 123,273."
  },
