    "md5": "ec229da1bf2e9f5dcdd6c3d60595f601",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1806,
    "chunk_text": "Yala National Park recorded the highest number of visitors during the six-month period, with more than 459,000 arrivals, followed by Udawalawe with 219,465, Horton Plains with 196,651 and the Elephant Transit Home with 123,273.\n\nThe park recorded 56,165 visitors in June alone, including 26,378 local visitors and 29,681 foreign visitors. Foreign visitors generated approximately Rs.305.27 million in revenue during the month.\n\nForeign visitor fees accounted for the largest share of wildlife tourism revenue during the first six months, generating approximately Rs.4.06 billion. Local visitor fees generated around Rs.94.5 million, while SAARC visitor fees contributed approximately Rs.27.5 million.\n\nUdawalawe National Park was the second-highest revenue earner, generating approximately Rs.927.5 million, while Minneriya generated around Rs.297.9 million.\n\nHorton Plains and Mirissa generated approximately Rs.264.3 million and Rs.233.4 million, respectively.\n\nThe Wildlife Department said the decision to close Yala Blocks 1 and 2 was aimed at reducing pressure on wildlife and the ecosystem during the difficult weather conditions.\n\nSri Lanka has 23 national parks, many of which attract large numbers of local and foreign visitors throughout the year. Several of the country's most popular wildlife destinations are located in dry-zone areas that are particularly vulnerable to prolonged dry weather and high temperatures.\n\nWith the dry season continuing, authorities face the challenge of maintaining tourism activities while ensuring that wildlife has adequate access to water and other essential resources.\n\nOfficials stressed that the protection of wildlife and fragile ecosystems would remain the priority, even if worsening weather conditions require wider temporary closures of national parks."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
