    "md5": "89dee0d494a338f03f84811e7aeccfcf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1200,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign reserves climb to five-month high of US$ 6.9bn on record Central Bank dollar purchases\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s gross official reserves rose to a provisional US$ 6,905 million by end-August 2026, the highest level in five months, as the Central Bank purchased a record volume of dollars from the domestic market amid strong remittance inflows.\n\nThe Central Bank\u2019s latest data showed that the stock increased by US$ 305 million or 4.6 percent from US$ 6,600 million at end-July, marking the strongest reading since end-March. The figure includes proceeds from the People\u2019s Bank of China swap arrangement, which the Central Bank has noted remains subject to conditionalities on usability. Reserves nevertheless remain US$ 365 million below the February peak of US$ 7,270 million, when the buffer crossed the US$ 7 billion mark for the first time in five and a half years.\n\nForeign currency reserves accounted for US$ 6,690 million of the August total, while gold holdings were valued at US$ 210 million, up from US$ 192 million in July.\n\nThe stock covers about 3.3 months of imports, measured against average monthly merchandise imports of US$ 2,092 million during the first seven months of the year."
