    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-news/CMTA-urges-policy-stability-demands-level-playing-field/273-350239",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789367435.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-14-dailymirrorlk-af364c4e-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "dc8a6184272113ad4bf9c30366b26f50",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "CMTA urges policy stability, demands level playing field\n\nThe Ceylon Motor Traders\u2019 Association (CMTA) has urged the government to maintain uniform and stable policies for the automobile sector, so that corporates can plan their businesses for the long run and attract foreign investments.\n\nFormer CMTA Chairman Charaka Perera said successive governments have changed the tax structure or the duty structure of the motor industry almost on an annual basis.\n\n\u201cThis is quite evident if you look back 10 to 12 years, and that has created volatility and instability for the trade where we all operate.\n\n\u201cIf you run through the changes, one could get an idea of what had taken place in the last decade.\n\n\u201cIn 2014, the duty on hybrids and EVs was drastically reduced, which created a heavy influx of EVs into the country when the market was not ready. In 2015, to counter that, the duty was reduced for petrol vehicles. Then in 2016, the duty was increased on hybrids and EVs, because the influx was too heavy. In 2018, the whole duty system was changed from value-based to capacity-based. In 2019, due to the influx of high-end, expensive or low-capacity vehicles in a larger context, a luxury tax was introduced. And in mid-May this year, another 50 percent surcharge was imposed on vehicles for just three months, up to August 15.\n\n\u201cLikewise, almost every year, there were changes to the duty structure, which impacted the motor trade and the demand that existed for vehicles,\u201d Perera said at a media briefing held in Colombo last week.\n\n\u201cThis type of volatility and instability creates uncertainty in the minds of the consumer. When the consumer\u2019s mind is uncertain, obviously, it impacts the sale. Companies operating in this trade find it very difficult to project sales and orders, which creates overstocking or sometimes a rush of imports, just to take advantage of duty changes,\u201d he emphasised."
  },
