    "md5": "e4c6f488776284374acf852f2fe357fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1997,
    "chunk_text": "CAA conducts over 1,000 raids on electrical, electronic goods\n\nColombo, Sep. 14 (Daily Mirror) - The Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) has conducted more than 1,000 island-wide raids on electrical and electronic goods so far this year, including 204 raids carried out under a recent special programme.\n\nThe special raids were conducted from August 25 to 29 and were resumed on September 8 and 9 based on information gathered during the initial operations.\n\nThe CAA said more than 1,150 electrical and electronic items were seized as case material or taken into official custody during the 204 raids, with legal action being initiated against those responsible.\n\nThe raids focused on products including extension cords, multi-sockets, plug tops, switch sockets, power cords, LED bulbs, chargers, electrical adapters, torches, electric scales, kettles, irons and power tools.\n\nOfficials also inspected whether traders maintained proper bills, receipts and invoices, could establish the source of their products and complied with labelling, product information and warranty requirements.\n\nThe CAA particularly stressed the requirement for a minimum six-month warranty for relevant electrical and electronic goods and said consumers should be provided with a written warranty certificate clearly specifying the warranty period, conditions and responsible party.\n\nThe authority said investigations would not necessarily stop at the retailer level. Under relevant laws, including Order No. 83, officials would trace the supply chain from retailer to supplier, distributor, importer or manufacturer where necessary.\n\nThose found to have been involved in offences could face legal action, including suppliers, distributors, importers and manufacturers, in addition to retailers.\n\nThe CAA urged traders to maintain proper invoices and documents identifying suppliers and importers, sell only goods complying with mandatory standards and labelling requirements, and provide the required warranty certificate."
  },
