    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9785bf9a33a5236f4eb51b5ad82d0525",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1736,
    "chunk_text": "EY urges IRD to expedite cases ahead of Dec. 2\n\nEY has called on the Inland Revenue Department (IRD) to expedite the pending tax audits, reviews and penalty-related decisions to ensure the taxpayers are not prevented by the administrative delays from accessing a time-bound interest waiver that expires on December 2.\n\nThe professional services firm said faster processing would be critical as the taxpayers seeking relief must settle both the principal tax liability and any applicable penalties within the stipulated six-month period to qualify for the waiver.\n\nUnder Section 42 of Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act No. 11 of 2026, the commissioner-general is required to write off the outstanding interest on the underpayments and late payments relating to the Income Tax, Surcharge Tax and Debt Repayment Levy, subject to the prescribed conditions.\n\nThe amendment was certified on June 3, giving the taxpayers until December 2 to make all qualifying payments.\n\nEY cautioned that practical difficulties could arise where the taxpayers have sought reductions in penalties and are still awaiting decisions from the IRD. Any penalty ultimately determined must also be paid before the deadline for the taxpayer to qualify for the interest waiver.\n\nIt said the IRD should therefore deal expeditiously with the penalty reduction requests and other outstanding matters that could affect the taxpayers\u2019 ability to complete the required payments within the relief period.\n\n\u201cThe purpose of the measure is to facilitate the collection of the outstanding revenue,\u201d EY said, adding that this would be best achieved through an efficient administrative process that allows the taxpayers to regularise their affairs before the concession expires."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
